{"causes": ["The ongoing Western Schism created a critical need for diplomatic and ecclesiastical figures loyal to the Avignon Papacy, providing an opportunity for Zalba's elevation.", "Navarre's strategic geographical position between France and Castile necessitated skilled chancellors to maintain its independence and diplomatic balance.", "King Charles II of Navarre recognized Zalba's administrative acumen, appointing him chancellor in 1376 and bishop of Pamplona shortly thereafter."], "effects": ["Martín de Zalba's chancellorship strengthened Navarre's diplomatic position and contributed to its political stability during the late 14th century.", "His cardinalate by Clement VII enhanced the Avignon Papacy's legitimacy and influence within the Iberian Peninsula.", "Zalba participated in key discussions and efforts aimed at resolving the Western Schism, though it persisted beyond his lifetime."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "martin-de-zalba", "sourceName": "Martín de Zalba", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "charles-iii-of-navarre", "targetName": "Charles III of Navarre", "context": "Martín de Zalba served as chancellor to King Charles III of Navarre, advising him on domestic and foreign policy from 1387 to 1396."}, {"sourceSlug": "western-schism", "sourceName": "Western Schism", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "martin-de-zalba", "targetName": "Martín de Zalba", "context": "The Western Schism directly led to Martín de Zalba's elevation as a cardinal by Antipope Clement VII in 1390, defining a major aspect of his later career."}, {"sourceSlug": "martin-de-zalba", "sourceName": "Martín de Zalba", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "western-schism", "targetName": "Western Schism", "context": "As a cardinal of the Avignon obedience from 1390, Martín de Zalba was an active participant in the political and religious dynamics of the Western Schism."}, {"sourceSlug": "martin-de-zalba", "sourceName": "Martín de Zalba", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "pamplona-spain", "targetName": "Pamplona, Spain", "context": "Martín de Zalba served as the bishop of Pamplona from 1377 until his death in 1403, making the city a primary center of his ecclesiastical and administrative activities."}, {"sourceSlug": "clement-vii-antipope", "sourceName": "Clement VII (Antipope)", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "martin-de-zalba", "targetName": "Martín de Zalba", "context": "Antipope Clement VII elevated Martín de Zalba to the College of Cardinals in 1390, strengthening the Avignon Papacy's influence in Navarre."}], "places": [{"name": "Pamplona, Spain", "role": "Bishopric, Administrative Center"}, {"name": "Avignon, France", "role": "Papal Seat, Cardinalate Location"}, {"name": "Navarre, Spain", "role": "Kingdom Served, Political Base"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Martín de Zalba", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "martín-de-zalba"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q938808", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mart%C3%ADn_de_Zalba"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Martín de Zalba's dual role as chancellor of Navarre and cardinal of the Avignon Papacy significantly influenced Navarrese foreign policy and provided crucial support for one side of the Western Schism, impacting regional politics and religious allegiances during a tumultuous era.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-05T09:15:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42000232247", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Martín de Zalba was a Navarrese cleric and statesman who served as the chancellor of the kingdom from 1376 until 1396 and as the bishop of Pamplona from 1377 until his death. From 1390, he was also a cardinal of the Avignon obedience.", "newValue": "Martín de Zalba (died 1403) emerged as a pivotal Navarrese cleric and statesman during the tumultuous late 14th century, a period defined by the Hundred Years' War and the profound rupture of the Western Schism. Serving as the chancellor of the Kingdom of Navarre from 1376 to 1396 and concurrently as the bishop of Pamplona from 1377 until his death, Zalba was a towering figure in both secular administration and ecclesiastical affairs. His elevation to cardinal by Antipope Clement VII in 1390 solidified his significance, making him a key player in the Avignon obedience and a crucial diplomatic asset for Navarre amidst the complex political landscape of medieval Iberia and Europe.

His chancellorship under King Charles II and later Charles III was marked by astute diplomacy and administrative competence. Zalba was instrumental in navigating Navarre's precarious position between the powerful kingdoms of France and Castile, consistently working to preserve the kingdom's independence and territorial integrity. As bishop of Pamplona, he oversaw significant church reforms and maintained the ecclesiastical infrastructure, while his cardinalate involved him directly in the high-stakes efforts to heal the papal schism, participating in councils and negotiations aimed at unifying the Church.

Zalba's dual role as a high-ranking cleric and a supreme secular administrator ensured that his influence rippled through both the temporal and spiritual domains of Navarre. His diplomatic efforts contributed significantly to the kingdom's stability during a period of widespread conflict, securing its borders and fostering economic relations. Furthermore, his steadfast loyalty to the Avignon Papacy, even amidst growing calls for a unified papacy, provided vital support for Clement VII's cause in the Iberian Peninsula, influencing the allegiances of other regional powers."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-05T09:15:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42000232247", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1337, Pamplona"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1337, Pamplona"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Western Schism created a critical need for diplomatic and ecclesiastical figures loyal to the Avignon Papacy, providing an opportunity for Zalba's elevation.", "Navarre's strategic geographical position between France and Castile necessitated skilled chancellors to maintain its independence and diplomatic balance.", "King Charles II of Navarre recognized Zalba's administrative acumen, appointing him chancellor in 1376 and bishop of Pamplona shortly thereafter."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-05T09:15:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42000232247", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1403-11-05"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1403-11-05"}], "newValue": ["Martín de Zalba's chancellorship strengthened Navarre's diplomatic position and contributed to its political stabil